From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Birk Skyum <birk.skyum@pm.me>
Date: Fri, 20 Nov 2026 00:40:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: qcom: sc8180x-microsoft-surface-pro-x: Keep the
 firmware's screen

On Omarchy's 7.3 the display clock controller sets up the PLL again
that the firmware's picture runs on, so the screen goes black about 37
seconds into the initramfs. The display driver could take over, but it
brings up the Adreno 680, and the machine resets when the GPU starts.

Turn off the display clock controller, the display subsystem and the
GPU, so the firmware's framebuffer keeps the screen. An SQ1 runs this
way with dispcc_sm8250 kept from loading, which has the same effect
for the clock controller. The tree change has not run on the tablet.

---
 .../boot/dts/qcom/sc8180x-microsoft-surface-pro-x.dtsi | 13 ++++++++++---
 1 file changed, 10 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sc8180x-microsoft-surface-pro-x.dtsi b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sc8180x-microsoft-surface-pro-x.dtsi
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sc8180x-microsoft-surface-pro-x.dtsi
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sc8180x-microsoft-surface-pro-x.dtsi
@@ -387,8 +387,15 @@
 	};
 };
 
+/*
+ * The display clock controller sets up the PLL the firmware's picture runs
+ * on again, and the screen goes black until the display driver takes over.
+ * That driver needs the GPU, whose start resets this machine on Omarchy's
+ * kernel. So the display stack stays off and the firmware's framebuffer
+ * keeps the screen.
+ */
 &dispcc {
-	status = "okay";
+	status = "disabled";
 };
 
 &gcc {
@@ -606,7 +613,7 @@
 };
 
 &gpu {
-	status = "okay";
+	status = "disabled";
 };
 
 &gpu_zap_shader {
@@ -614,7 +621,7 @@
 };
 
 &mdss {
-	status = "okay";
+	status = "disabled";
 };
 
 &mdss_edp {
-- 
2.47.0

